{"action": "diagnose", "why": "The tool results support the investigator's cause, and the seven duplicates add up exactly to the $493.23 gap, so the alert is explained and a rollback of ledger-consumer fits.", "diagnosis": {"hypothesis": "ledger-consumer was recreated at 16:21:50Z with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive. That handler INSERTs a new transaction for every provider-events message and never checks whether the event_id was already booked. The provider redelivers webhooks, and webhook-receiver publishes each delivery as its own Kafka message. So each redelivery after 16:21:50Z became a second or third balanced ledger posting. ledger_balanced still passes, but posted_exactly_once fails on 7 events and matches_provider fails on the provider_clearing total and on each affected payment. The duplicates net exactly to the reported gap: 3709 + 9533 + 4444 + 1827 + 5879 + 2×13193 = 51778 cents. The duplicated refund of 2455 cents is subtracted, giving 49323 cents, or +$493.23. payment-confirmer is idempotent (already_confirmed), so payments and refunds still match the provider.", "evidence": [{"call_id": "c1", "shows": "posted_exactly_once failed. Six events were posted twice and evt_000308 ($131.93) three times. The amounts net to +49323 cents, matching matches_provider's +$493.23 (ledger 1550150 vs provider 1500827). pay_000217 is -2455, from the doubled refund evt_000288. ledger_balanced, payments_match_provider and refunds_match_provider passed. Duplicate transaction ids run from 251 to 297."}, {"call_id": "c2", "shows": "ledger-consumer was recreated at 16:21:50Z with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, 17s before the alert. Every other service was created at 16:20:23Z and has not changed since."}, {"call_id": "c7", "shows": "evt_000251 was posted at 16:21:50.833Z (2@73) and again at 16:21:51.444Z (2@76), right after the recreate. Its transactions are 251 and 255, the lowest duplicate ids, so the other duplicates (ids 262 to 297) fall later, assuming ids are sequential."}, {"call_id": "c3", "shows": "ledger-consumer logged 'posted' for evt_000308 three times, from offsets 0@100, 0@101 and 0@103, between 16:22:04.3Z and 16:22:05.8Z."}, {"call_id": "c4", "shows": "webhook-receiver queued evt_000308 three times, once per delivery, at offsets 100, 101 and 103. Redeliveries reach Kafka as separate messages."}, {"call_id": "c5", "shows": "For offset 101, payment-confirmer returned already_confirmed. ledger-consumer returned posted, and its only queries were INSERTs into transactions and entries, with no lookup of the event_id first."}, {"call_id": "c8", "shows": "Before the recreate, ledger-consumer logged 'already processed' for repeats. The last one is at 16:21:48.605Z, and there are none after 16:21:50Z in a window that runs to 16:22:48Z."}, {"call_id": "c6", "shows": "The ledger already_processed rate falls off after the recreate while posted rises. The rate values are 1m extrapolated increases, so the tail values still reflect skips from before the recreate."}], "confidence": 0.87, "proposed_fix": {"kind": "rollback", "target": "ledger-consumer", "summary": "Roll ledger-consumer back to the settings it ran before the 16:21:50Z deploy. That should be the deduplicating handler, but c2 does not show the earlier value of LEDGER_HANDLER; the deploy log does. This stops new duplicate postings. As a separate, human-run data repair, reverse or remove the extra transactions: 255, 266, 272, 273 and 297, the later of each pair for evt_000298, and two of the three for evt_000308 (ids not listed in c1). Then rerun the money check."}, "blast_radius": "The ledger-consumer container is recreated, so ledger posting pauses briefly and resumes from its committed offsets. payment-confirmer, payments-api and webhook-receiver are not touched. If the earlier settings were also bad in some way, that behaviour comes back; the earlier value of LEDGER_HANDLER has not been seen. The rollback does not repair the 8 duplicate postings already in the ledger, so the money check keeps failing until the data repair is done.", "rollback_plan": "Recreate ledger-consumer with the current settings (LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, BAD_EVENTS=dead_letter) through a normal deploy. Avoid this unless the rollback breaks posting entirely, because naive mode books every redelivery again.", "fix_action": {"name": "rollback_deploy", "arguments": {"service": "ledger-consumer"}}}}
```

